<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakhon Pathom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nakhon Pathom is a town west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and one of the largest centres of the Dvaravati culture between the 7th and 11th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> coins found there and at other sites, inscribed with the merit of the king of Dvaravati, gave that culture its name. The town is dominated by the Phra Pathom Chedi, among the tallest stupas in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakhon-pathom.jpg" alt="Nakhon Pathom" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Preecha.MJ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0004932_-_(Phra_Pathom_Chedi_-_001).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Revire sees Funan as the catalyst for Dvāravatī in central Thailand, where similar artefacts occur, although Stephen Murphy warns that the ceramics of <b>Nakhon Pathom</b> show trade contact only.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Wild Tiger Corps and Siamese scouts first sang it on 22 January 1914 at Ban Bo Suphan, on a march from <b>Nakhon Pathom</b> to the pagoda at Don Chedi, and the Ministry of Education adopted it for schools nationwide that year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li><li><blockquote>Dvaravati takes its name from silver coins found at <b>Nakhon Pathom</b> and other sites inscribed 'meritorious deeds of the king of Dvaravati', matching the Duoluobodi of Chinese pilgrims.</blockquote><a href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a></li></ul></section>
